==English==

===Etymology===
Back-formation from {{der|en|enm|part-takinge}}, {{m|enm|part-takynge|t=a sharing; partaking}}, a calque of Latin {{m|la|particeps|t=participating}}; equivalent to {{com|en|part|take}}. Compare {{m|en|take part}}. In the sense of taking a share or portion of something, displaced native {{ncog|ang|onbītan||to taste of, to partake of}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|pär-tāk'|a=GenAm}}, {{IPA|en|/pɑɹˈteɪk/}}
** {{audio|en|en-us-partake.ogg|a=US}}
* {{enPR|pär-tāk'|a=RP}}, {{IPA|en|/pɑːˈteɪk/}}
* {{rhymes|en|eɪk|s=2}}
* {{hyphenation|en|par|take}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|||partook|partaken}} {{tlb|en|intransitive}}

# {{lb|en|formal}} To take part in an activity; to [[participate]]. {{+obj|en|:in}}
#* {{RQ:Locke Human Understanding|text=Brutes '''partake''' in this faculty.}}
# {{lb|en|formal}} To take a [[share]] or [[portion]]. {{+obj|en|:of/:in}}
#: {{ux|en|Will you '''partake''' of some food?}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1922|author=[[w:Agatha Christie|Agatha Christie]]|title=The Secret Adversary|chapter=[[s:The Secret Adversary/Chapter 17|Chapter 17]]|passage=The steak and chips '''partaken''' of for lunch seemed now to belong to another decade. He regretfully recognized the fact that he would not make a success of a hunger strike.}}
# {{senseid|en|archaic}} {{lb|en|archaic}} To have something of the properties, character, or office. {{+obj|en|:of}}
#* '''c. 1620''', {{w|Francis Bacon}}, letter of advice to Sir {{w|George Villiers, 1st Duke of Buckingham|George Villiers}}
#*: the Attorney of the Duchy of Lancaster , who '''partakes''' of both qualities, partly of a judge in the court, and partly of an attorney-general
#* {{quote-book|en|2001|David L. Lieber; Jules Harlow|Etz Hayim: Torah and Commentary||1107|The people are encouraged to perceive their liberation in historic terms and to trust that new events will '''partake''' of past glories.}}

